WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:01.430
Harvest now,

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:05.705
decrypt later is the risk that an attacker collects encrypted traffic or stored encrypted

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.820
data today and saves it for possible decryption in the future.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.035
The attacker may not be able to read the data now.

00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:12.835
The concern is that future capability,

00:00:12.835 --> 00:00:15.560
including cryptographically relevant quantum capability,

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.015
could expose data whose confidentiality still matters years from now.

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:21.280
This risk depends heavily on data lifetime.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.300
If information only needs to remain confidential for minutes or days,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.080
future decryption years later may have limited value.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.265
If information must remain confidential for decades, the risk is much more important.

00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:35.420
Government data, healthcare records, legal records, intellectual property, trade secrets,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.910
critical infrastructure details,

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:40.720
and sensitive personal information may all have long value windows.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.760
Encrypted network traffic is one area of concern,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.810
especially when sessions depend on quantum-vulnerable public-key key exchange.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.395
Stored encrypted data is another.

00:00:48.395 --> 00:00:51.470
Backups, archives, database dumps, document repositories,

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:55.685
and captured files may remain sensitive long after the original system is retired.

00:00:55.685 --> 00:00:59.400
Security teams should understand which data has long confidentiality requirements and

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.585
where encryption depends on vulnerable key establishment.

00:01:01.585 --> 00:01:04.480
The response is not panic collection of new tools.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.675
It is prioritization.

00:01:05.675 --> 00:01:09.020
Start with data that has long sensitivity, high adversary interest,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.360
and exposure to collection.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.715
Then identify the systems, protocols, certificates, keys, and vendors involved.

00:01:14.715 --> 00:01:18.910
Harvest-now-decrypt-later risk becomes manageable when it is tied to concrete data classes

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.960
and migration plans rather than treated as a vague future worry.
